you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme a promise that  november's climate summit will be the  most inclusive cop  yet as the government reveals the main  themes for the meeting  slow progress on fast fashion 250 of the  world's biggest brands and retailers are  criticized  for not being open enough about their  environmental practices  and how a new type of tire could help  cut  air pollution and microplastics  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions now in glasgow in november a  12-day summit will take place  and the decisions that are made there  will affect the future of the whole  planet  world leaders activists business  representatives and scientists will all  be attending  cop 26 the most important climate  meeting  in six years and today we heard the  government's latest ambitions  for that summit young people will  interview government ministers on stage  as part of plans to make cop 26 the most  inclusive ever  while other themes include nature gender  adaptation  and cities well boris johnson has been  questioned by  mps today about the government's plans  for cop our chief political  correspondent john craig was listening  so john what did he say more questions  than answers really  quite techy exchanges between the prime  minister and  indeed particularly some conservative  mps on the committee philip dunn  caroline knox and tom tougenhart in  particular  he was asked how the government intended  to meet all its pledges  on climate change he was even asked by  caroline knox  how is the cop summit going to be gender  specific he kept stressing however on  measures to tackle climate change that  he was determined to keep  bills low for energy consumers this is a  government  determined to keep bills low he said at  one point  eco-friendly heat pumps he said cost ten  thousand pounds  that's a lot of money for ordinary  people he said  uh it was uh pretty pretty spiky and  spicy at times  at one point tom tugan are no love lost  there  said i'm interested in your answers to  my questions  not ones that you wish i had asked he  said  so we didn't learn much specifically  from the prime minister there  and it was all a bit awkward and a bit  spicy  john thank you let's take a look now at  the day's other climate news and  campaigners want the uk  to invest in new technologies to recover  raw materials from  e-waste a new study by the  not-for-profit organization  material focus suggests 379  000 kilograms of materials like gold  silver and platinum  are found each year in electricals which  have been thrown away  rather than recycled it estimates a  proportion of these metals worth at  least  13 million pounds can't be retrieved  through the uk's existing  mining processes a river in the lake  district will be  re-wiggled to reduce flooding and  restore  wildlife populations the national trust  has begun work to recreate a series of  bends and smaller channels in gold real  back  which has been straightened over  hundreds of years it's hoped that this  will  slow the flow of water downstream and  reconnect the river with a flood plain  allowing the wider landscape to absorb  the water  the woodland trust says it will phase  out using plastic tree guards at its  sites by the end of this year in an  effort to be more sustainable  the charity have been trialling a wide  range of plastic-free alternatives  including cardboard and wool  to see how effective they are tree  guards are used to prevent  deer and rabbits from grazing on  saplings in the early stages of growth  and a french company has developed a  robot that eats  rubbish from the sea the electric  powered boat called the jellyfish bot  uses a net to collect plastic bottles  bags and other waste found in harbours  that human cleaners can't reach it's  already in operation in 15 french ports  and is being exported to  singapore japan and norway  more than a hundred billion pieces of  clothing are made  every year around the world and the  fashion industry is responsible  for around 10 percent of global  emissions  but it can be really hard to know what  shops are doing to protect the  environment or  improve their sustainability the fashion  transparency index ranks 250 of the  world's largest brands and retailers  and in a moment we'll be hearing about  their latest findings  but first here's your guide to fast  fashion in 60 seconds  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  well let's bring in sarah ditty global  policy director at fashion revolution  an activism movement founded after the  rana plaza factory collapse  in 2013. sarah ditty hello to you  the impact of fast fashion is being  talked about more isn't it but  how much are big brands doing to try to  reduce  their environmental impact  well overall the standout finding of the  fashion transparency index this year is  that the world's biggest fashion brands  are moving too  slowly on transparency and that needs to  change  especially when it comes to really  important issues such as textile waste  carbon emissions and fair pay for  workers in the supply chain and i think  it's important to highlight that  most big brands are failing to disclose  their supply chain carbon emissions  despite having made big public  commitments to go net zero over the past  couple of years and  and we really want to encourage brands  to be more transparent about this  especially given the urgency of the  climate crisis  well yes and is that purely voluntary at  this stage because i noticed that one  number that really stood out in your  report  is that 95 of brands don't disclose  how much water they use i mean how much  pressure is  on them to be transparent about  statistics like that  yeah unfortunately it's it's not an  obligation on brands to  disclose their water footprint even  though we know that clothing production  uses a huge amount of water in areas  where there is severe  water scarcity but you know you can't  manage what you don't measure  and this is true for lots of other  environmental impacts to the legislation  just  isn't there yet to ensure and require  brands to be open and transparent about  the serious environmental impacts they  are creating in the global fashion value  chain  would you like to see legislation or do  you think that consumers can provide the  pressure  needed you know we need both  we as part of this research process we  we meet with big brands and  whenever we do we ask them what's going  to make them change  and they always tell us that when their  customers ask them to do more  they will and even if they get 100  emails  from their customers this makes a big  difference so whether you shop with  certain brands or not  you should we should all be using our  voice to challenge big brands to do more  about their social and environmental  impacts but tougher legislation is also  crucial  and in the index we've seen a number of  big brands refuse to be transparent over  the past few years of doing this  research  and we know that legislation is the only  thing that's going to make them move  to do the right thing sarah ditty thanks  very much indeed  thank you so much now you're probably  very familiar with the pollution  associated with exhaust  fumes from petrol and diesel cars but  tires could actually be doing more harm  to our health and the environment  now producing tyres in the first place  has a huge climate impact from  deforestation  to the use of fossil fuels to make  synthetic rubber  and as vehicles move around the tires  gradually wear down  producing particulate pollution well  some estimates put that pollution at  around a thousand times  higher than what comes out of a car's  exhaust pipe  now this means more air pollution  especially in cities  and as tyres are used they also produce  tiny plastic  polymers and according to one study  around 28 percent  of micro plastic pollution in the oceans  comes  from tyres tyre pollution is an even  bigger problem for  electric vehicles batteries make them  heavier  putting more pressure on the wheels and  evs have  instant torque meaning much faster  acceleration  wearing towers more quickly well as part  of an initiative to clean up london's  air tfl launched the london freight lab  innovation challenge  sustainable tire developer enso was one  of the winners and they've created what  they say  is a revolutionary new tyre that  increases the range of electric vehicles  and reduces pollution  well their founder and chief executive  gun lawyer ellinson  joins me now so tell us a little bit  about this new tire of yours how does it  differ  from a traditional tire thank you very  much for having us we're very proud here  to be here representing enzo so we've  developed a tire that is energy  efficient  durable and more sustainable than its  counterparts from  the wider tire industry and we're doing  this  basically for the reasons you  demonstrate not only our tire pollution  now more particularly matter in our air  that comes from tailpipes but we also  have this problem that we are simply  increasing the amount of microplastic  pollution that is in our oceans and then  there we breathe  our tires are designed to have more  bio-based and low-carbon raw materials  they're designed to be  more energy efficient so we can also  extend the range  of those electric cars and we're  partnered now with dpd  as part of london freight lab which is  an opportunity to test our tires on  dpd's electric vans  which is an initiative supported by tfl  and the mayor of london  and this not only gives us an  opportunity both to repo reduce  dpd's environmental impact but also  giving their ev  drivers more range for each drive they  have  and what are the challenges been in  developing an environmentally friendly  tire  i think one of the biggest development  hurdles has  actually not been on the entire  development side but simply convincing  people that there's a better way forward  but  we're very fortunate we recently secured  investment from the uk government  actually  and we now actually sourced over 800  private investors  on our crowd cube platform on crowdcube  which is still open and open until  friday  and ultimately it's building these  partnerships that are very different  from how the rest of the tire industry  operates we were very  collegiate we have collaborations now  with major ev tired  so image ev car makers and major ee  fleets  and this is simply a demonstration that  the the industry the wider automotive  industry sees this as a problem and  wants to solve it  while the rest of the tower industry i  would say is fairly reluctant to even  admit it has a problem  good lawyer ellingson thanks very much  indeed thank you so much  well that's it from us for today  tomorrow how using nature can help  tackle the climate crisis  from super sea grass to kelp forests and  oysters  you can find out more tomorrow here on  the daily climate show thanks for  watching  see you then

